  "texts": [
    "How does a thermos keep tea hot for hours?",
    "Heat always flows from hot to cold.",
    "A normal mug loses it in three ways.",
    "First, conduction: jiggling molecules pass heat into the wall.",
    "Second, convection: warm air rises and carries heat away.",
    "Third, radiation: every warm thing gives off infrared light.",
    "A thermos has two walls, one inside the other.",
    "Between them is a vacuum, almost no air at all.",
    "No molecules means no conduction and no convection.",
    "But radiation crosses a vacuum, like sunlight through space.",
    "That's why the walls are silvered like a mirror.",
    "The infrared bounces right back into the tea.",
    "The weak spot is the stopper, where most heat escapes.",
    "So a good thermos keeps tea warm for six hours.",
    "It works backwards too: iced lemonade stays cold.",
    "Follow us to find more physics in your kitchen!"
  ]
